<urn:atm:triage:finding/AV3-B2>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AV3-B2" ;
  triage:title "Python/Rust read-handler name divergence: lint gate hard-fails at its own AV.1b activation moment" ;
  triage:description ".just/check_read_concurrency_gates.py:31 has READ_HANDLERS = (\"list\", \"peek\", \"read\", \"doctor\"); the real router fns in storage_and_nudge_router.rs are list_messages (:493), peek_messages (:514), receive_messages (:536), doctor (:579) — the names the Rust test itself uses. `fn list(`/`fn peek(`/`fn read(` occur nowhere in the router. When AV.1b lands the AsyncMailboxRuntime marker, extract_fn_body raises ValueError(\"read handler `list` is missing\") and the gate exits 1 on a scanner bug instead of scanning. The checker's unit-test fixtures use the wrong names too (.just/tests/test_check_read_concurrency_gates.py:54-57), which is why dev validation passed. Fix: correct the tuple AND add a parity test pinning the Python and Rust handler-name lists to one source of truth. Reviewer ids ARCH-AV3-4, RBQA-AV3-003." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-av" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "correctness" ;
  triage:severity "blocking" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope ".just/*.py scanners and .just/tests fixtures and crates/atm-architecture/tests/boundary_enforcement.rs for every hardcoded handler/function-name list referring to the storage_and_nudge_router read handlers" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-08-31T06:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AV3 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-08-31T05:33:39Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AV3-B2/AV3-B2-1> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AV3-B2/AV3-B2-2> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AV3-B2/AV3-B2-3> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AV3-B2/AV3-B2-4> ;
  triage:triageRecordVersion "1" .

